SHC2 - radio co-processor of the RWE / innogy SmartHome Controller 2.0 (SHC2)

The SHC2 main board (Actions S500) carries a plug-in extension board (S500B-EXT) with an
ATmega328P and a CC110L module (26 MHz reference, 868 MHz). The S500 talks to it over a
UART and programs it in-circuit. This device target makes that radio part run culfw.
It is a copy of Devices/SHC (SHC v1) - same pin-out, different host and baud rate.

HARDWARE (measured 2026-10-21)

  MCU              ATmega328P, 11.0592 MHz crystal
  Transceiver      CC110L, 26 MHz reference (PARTNUM 0x00, VERSION 0x17)
  CC110L CS        PB2   (pulled up on the board)
  CC110L GDO0      PD2   (default CLK_XOSC/192 toggles there after power-on)
  CC110L GDO2      PD3   -> RX interrupt is INT1
  SPI              PB3 / PB4 / PB5 (MOSI / MISO / SCK), shared with the host
  Serial           57600 8N1 on the ATmega UART, wired to the host UART0 (ttyS0)
  LED              none

HOST SIDE (S500)

  - Supply: S500 GPIO 36 switches the 5 V EN line of the extension. Keep it on.
  - ISP: RESET = S500 GPIO 28, SCK/MOSI/MISO = S500 GPIO 72/71/70 (DSI pads).
  - While culfw runs, the S500 DSI pads must be high impedance: set GPIO INEN for
    GPIOC6..8 first, then the MFP fields to a digital function. In DSI mode the pads
    pull the SPI lines low and the ATmega cannot talk to the CC110L reliably.

CLOCK: the clib was written for an 8 MHz core. board.h sets CULFW_CORE_HZ = F_CPU, and
clib/timebase.h scales the SlowRF time base to 11.0592 MHz: Timer1 pulse measurement and
timeouts, my_delay_us(), and the Timer0 tick (125.58 Hz, +0.47 %). Verified 2026-10-21
against a CUL868 and with a HackRF: FS20 pulses 400/600 us, FS20 and AskSin in both
directions.

FACTORY STATE OF THE ATmega328P

The unit examined came with an empty flash and EEPROM (all 0xFF) and lock bits 0xFF.
Its fuses select the internal RC oscillator divided by 8: low 0x62, high 0xD9,
extended 0xFF. culfw needs the crystal, so set the low fuse to 0xFF before flashing.
Program EESAVE as well (high fuse 0xD9 -> 0xD1): as delivered it is unprogrammed, and
every chip erase clears the EEPROM along with the flash.

CRYSTAL CALIBRATION

As on a CUL, the FSCTRL0 value that puts the unit on frequency goes to EEPROM 0x3D8,
its complement to 0x3D9 (W03D8xx, W03D9yy), and every mode applies it. With EESAVE
programmed it survives the chip erase of later in-circuit flashes (checked: high fuse
0xD1, flash erased and rewritten, 0x3D8/0x3D9 unchanged); without EESAVE, write it
back after every flash. FSCTRL0 works on the CC110L: the unit examined sent 2.2 kHz
below 868.3 MHz with FSCTRL0 = 0 and 0.5 kHz below with the stored value 1.

BUILD

  make TARGET=shc2CUL868 mostly_clean build
  make TARGET=shc2CUL868 bin
